1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,761
In this lesson, we'll start integral
calculus by asking: how much has

2
00:00:04,761 --> 00:00:05,589
accumulated?

3
00:00:06,733 --> 00:00:11,745
Drive at sixty miles per hour for two
hours, and you travel one hundred twenty

4
00:00:11,745 --> 00:00:12,131
miles.

5
00:00:12,581 --> 00:00:17,466
On a velocity graph, that's the area of a
rectangle: height sixty, width two.

6
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:23,699
When the rate changes, accumulated change
is still the area under the rate curve.

7
00:00:24,149 --> 00:00:30,304
If velocity rises steadily from zero to
forty over four seconds, the distance is a

8
00:00:30,304 --> 00:00:32,106
triangle's area: eighty.

9
00:00:33,233 --> 00:00:36,241
Area below the axis counts as negative
change.

10
00:00:36,691 --> 00:00:41,107
If water flows out, the volume decreases,
and that region subtracts.

11
00:00:42,233 --> 00:00:47,332
An accumulation function tracks the total
area from a starting point up to x.

12
00:00:47,782 --> 00:00:52,674
As x moves right, the accumulated area
grows when the rate is positive and

13
00:00:52,674 --> 00:00:54,459
shrinks when it's negative.

14
00:00:55,600 --> 00:01:00,251
The units of accumulated change are rate
units times input units.

15
00:01:00,701 --> 00:01:03,367
Gallons per minute times minutes gives
gallons.

16
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:09,570
Your turn. A rate is three liters per
minute for five minutes, then one liter

17
00:01:09,570 --> 00:01:12,863
per minute for four minutes. How much
accumulated?

18
00:01:13,313 --> 00:01:14,849
Pause the video and try it.

19
00:01:15,299 --> 00:01:17,475
Fifteen plus four, nineteen liters.

20
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:22,995
In this lesson, we'll estimate the area
under a curve using rectangles.

21
00:01:24,133 --> 00:01:29,842
Split the interval into n subintervals of
width delta x. On each, build a rectangle

22
00:01:29,842 --> 00:01:33,349
whose height is the function's value at
some point.

23
00:01:33,799 --> 00:01:36,679
Add up the rectangle areas: that's a
Riemann sum.

24
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:43,755
Estimate the area under x squared from
zero to two with four rectangles. Delta x

25
00:01:43,755 --> 00:01:44,648
is one half.

26
00:01:45,098 --> 00:01:51,029
Left endpoints: one half times zero plus
one fourth plus one plus nine fourths,

27
00:01:51,029 --> 00:01:56,661
which is one point seven five. Right
endpoints give three point seven five.

28
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:01,192
The midpoint sum uses the center of each
subinterval.

29
00:02:01,642 --> 00:02:07,593
Heights at one fourth, three fourths, five
fourths and seven fourths give two point

30
00:02:07,593 --> 00:02:12,970
six two five. The true area is eight
thirds, about two point six six seven.

31
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:17,620
The trapezoidal rule averages the left and
right sums.

32
00:02:18,070 --> 00:02:20,097
Here, that's two point seven five.

33
00:02:21,233 --> 00:02:26,460
For an increasing function, left sums
underestimate and right sums overestimate.

34
00:02:26,910 --> 00:02:31,507
For a concave up function, the trapezoid
overestimates and the midpoint

35
00:02:31,507 --> 00:02:32,478
underestimates.

36
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:38,891
Your turn. Estimate the area under one
over x from one to three with two right

37
00:02:38,891 --> 00:02:39,637
rectangles.

38
00:02:40,087 --> 00:02:41,623
Pause the video and try it.

39
00:02:42,073 --> 00:02:45,828
Delta x is one: one half plus one third,
five sixths.

40
00:02:46,966 --> 00:02:52,321
In this lesson, we'll define the definite
integral as the exact accumulated area.

41
00:02:53,466 --> 00:02:58,351
As the number of rectangles goes to
infinity, Riemann sums approach a limit.

42
00:02:58,801 --> 00:03:02,172
That limit is the definite integral of f
from a to b.

43
00:03:03,299 --> 00:03:08,697
We write the integral sign, then f of x d
x, with a at the bottom and b at the top.

44
00:03:09,147 --> 00:03:12,411
The d x reminds us of the tiny widths
delta x.

45
00:03:13,533 --> 00:03:19,357
Some integrals are just geometry. The
integral from zero to three of two x is a

46
00:03:19,357 --> 00:03:22,749
triangle with base three and height six:
nine.

47
00:03:23,199 --> 00:03:29,210
The integral from negative two to two of
the square root of four minus x squared is

48
00:03:29,210 --> 00:03:31,817
half a circle of radius two: two pi.

49
00:03:32,966 --> 00:03:37,958
The integral gives signed area: area above
the axis minus area below.

50
00:03:38,408 --> 00:03:44,040
The integral of sine x from zero to two pi
is zero, since the two humps cancel.

51
00:03:45,166 --> 00:03:50,051
Integrals add over adjacent intervals, and
reversing limits flips the sign.

52
00:03:50,501 --> 00:03:55,088
Constants factor out, and the integral of
a sum is the sum of the integrals.

53
00:03:56,233 --> 00:04:01,928
Your turn. If the integral from zero to
five of f is eight and from zero to two of

54
00:04:01,928 --> 00:04:05,193
f is three, find the integral from two to
five.

55
00:04:05,643 --> 00:04:07,179
Pause the video and try it.

56
00:04:07,629 --> 00:04:09,570
Eight minus three, which is five.

57
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:15,450
In this lesson, we'll meet the Fundamental
Theorem of Calculus, which ties

58
00:04:15,450 --> 00:04:17,697
derivatives and integrals together.

59
00:04:18,833 --> 00:04:25,878
Part one: if g of x is the integral from a
to x of f of t d t, then g prime of x

60
00:04:25,878 --> 00:04:27,111
equals f of x.

61
00:04:27,561 --> 00:04:31,806
The rate at which accumulated area grows
is the height of the curve.

62
00:04:32,933 --> 00:04:38,385
The derivative of the integral from zero
to x of the square root of one plus t

63
00:04:38,385 --> 00:04:41,531
cubed is the square root of one plus x
cubed.

64
00:04:41,981 --> 00:04:47,015
With x squared as the upper limit, use the
chain rule: multiply by two x.

65
00:04:48,133 --> 00:04:54,647
Part two: if F is any antiderivative of f,
then the integral from a to b of f equals

66
00:04:54,647 --> 00:04:56,197
F of b minus F of a.

67
00:04:56,647 --> 00:05:00,274
To find an area, find a function whose
derivative is f.

68
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:06,882
The integral from zero to two of x
squared: an antiderivative is x cubed over

69
00:05:06,882 --> 00:05:07,309
three.

70
00:05:07,759 --> 00:05:13,114
Eight thirds minus zero is eight thirds,
exactly what our Riemann sums approached.

71
00:05:14,233 --> 00:05:19,135
Before this theorem, areas needed infinite
sums. Now they need only an

72
00:05:19,135 --> 00:05:20,185
antiderivative.

73
00:05:20,635 --> 00:05:23,899
Differentiation and integration undo each
other.

74
00:05:25,033 --> 00:05:29,257
Your turn. Evaluate the integral from one
to e of one over x.

75
00:05:29,707 --> 00:05:31,243
Pause the video and try it.

76
00:05:31,693 --> 00:05:34,253
ln e minus ln one, which is one.

77
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:39,027
In this lesson, we'll learn to run
differentiation backward.

78
00:05:40,167 --> 00:05:43,815
F is an antiderivative of f if F prime
equals f.

79
00:05:44,265 --> 00:05:46,974
x squared is an antiderivative of two x.

80
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:53,792
So is x squared plus five, or x squared
minus seven. Antiderivatives differ by

81
00:05:53,792 --> 00:05:54,521
constants.

82
00:05:54,971 --> 00:05:59,217
The indefinite integral of two x d x is x
squared plus C.

83
00:06:00,367 --> 00:06:06,946
The power rule in reverse: the integral of
x to the n is x to the n plus one, over n

84
00:06:06,946 --> 00:06:10,863
plus one, plus C, for n not equal to
negative one.

85
00:06:11,313 --> 00:06:15,878
The integral of x to the n is easy to
check: differentiate the answer.

86
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:23,724
The integral of one over x is ln of the
absolute value of x. The integral of e to

87
00:06:23,724 --> 00:06:25,384
the x is e to the x.

88
00:06:25,834 --> 00:06:32,634
The integral of cosine x is sine x, and of
sine x is negative cosine x, and of secant

89
00:06:32,634 --> 00:06:34,474
squared x is tangent x.

90
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:41,339
If f prime of x equals six x squared and f
of one equals five, find f.

91
00:06:41,789 --> 00:06:48,903
f equals two x cubed plus C. Then two plus
C equals five, so C equals three: f of x

92
00:06:48,903 --> 00:06:51,474
equals two x cubed plus three.

93
00:06:52,600 --> 00:06:58,424
Your turn. Find the integral of four x
cubed minus three over x plus e to the x.

94
00:06:58,874 --> 00:07:00,410
Pause the video and try it.

95
00:07:00,860 --> 00:07:05,980
x to the fourth minus three ln absolute x
plus e to the x, plus C.

96
00:07:07,100 --> 00:07:11,431
In this lesson, we'll learn substitution,
the reverse of the chain rule.

97
00:07:12,567 --> 00:07:19,338
If an integrand looks like f of g of x
times g prime of x, its antiderivative is

98
00:07:19,338 --> 00:07:20,353
F of g of x.

99
00:07:20,803 --> 00:07:25,881
We let u equal g of x, so d u equals g
prime of x d x.

100
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:32,359
Choose u as the inside of a composite,
especially one whose derivative also

101
00:07:32,359 --> 00:07:32,931
appears.

102
00:07:33,381 --> 00:07:39,077
In two x times x squared plus one to the
fifth, let u equal x squared plus one.

103
00:07:40,200 --> 00:07:45,683
Then d u equals two x d x, and the
integral becomes u to the fifth d u.

104
00:07:46,133 --> 00:07:51,387
That's u to the sixth over six, or x
squared plus one to the sixth over six,

105
00:07:51,387 --> 00:07:51,871
plus C.

106
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:58,613
For definite integrals, change the limits
too. The integral from zero to one of x e

107
00:07:58,613 --> 00:07:59,763
to the x squared.

108
00:08:00,213 --> 00:08:07,302
u equals x squared, so u runs from zero to
one, and x d x is half of d u. The answer

109
00:08:07,302 --> 00:08:09,834
is one half times e minus one.

110
00:08:10,967 --> 00:08:17,215
If a constant is missing, adjust. The
integral of cosine of three x: u equals

111
00:08:17,215 --> 00:08:19,649
three x, d u equals three d x.

112
00:08:20,099 --> 00:08:22,787
So it's one third of sine three x, plus C.

113
00:08:23,933 --> 00:08:27,240
Your turn. Integrate x over x squared plus
four.

114
00:08:27,690 --> 00:08:29,226
Pause the video and try it.

115
00:08:29,676 --> 00:08:35,287
u equals x squared plus four: one half ln
of x squared plus four, plus C.

116
00:08:36,433 --> 00:08:42,012
In this lesson, a BC topic, we'll learn
integration by parts, the reverse of the

117
00:08:42,012 --> 00:08:42,918
product rule.

118
00:08:44,066 --> 00:08:49,442
The integral of u d v equals u v minus the
integral of v d u.

119
00:08:49,892 --> 00:08:53,519
It trades one integral for another,
hopefully easier one.

120
00:08:54,666 --> 00:09:00,893
Choose u as something that simplifies when
differentiated. A guide: logs, inverse

121
00:09:00,893 --> 00:09:03,584
trig, algebraic, trig, exponential.

122
00:09:04,034 --> 00:09:06,018
Pick u from whichever comes first.

123
00:09:07,166 --> 00:09:14,916
For x e to the x: u equals x, d v equals e
to the x d x. Then d u equals d x and v

124
00:09:14,916 --> 00:09:16,617
equals e to the x.

125
00:09:17,067 --> 00:09:21,483
The integral equals x e to the x minus e
to the x, plus C.

126
00:09:22,633 --> 00:09:26,644
For ln x: u equals ln x, d v equals d x.

127
00:09:27,094 --> 00:09:32,683
x ln x minus the integral of one, so x ln
x minus x, plus C.

128
00:09:33,833 --> 00:09:36,692
For x squared sine x, apply parts twice.

129
00:09:37,142 --> 00:09:44,037
The result is negative x squared cosine x
plus two x sine x plus two cosine x, plus

130
00:09:44,037 --> 00:09:44,203
C.

131
00:09:45,333 --> 00:09:47,808
Your turn. Integrate x cosine x.

132
00:09:48,258 --> 00:09:49,794
Pause the video and try it.

133
00:09:50,244 --> 00:09:57,028
u equals x, d v equals cosine x d x: x
sine x plus cosine x, plus C.

134
00:09:58,166 --> 00:10:03,230
In this lesson, a BC topic, we'll
integrate rational functions by splitting

135
00:10:03,230 --> 00:10:05,121
them into simpler fractions.

136
00:10:06,266 --> 00:10:12,036
When the denominator factors into distinct
linear factors, partial fractions turns

137
00:10:12,036 --> 00:10:14,991
one hard integral into easy log integrals.

138
00:10:15,441 --> 00:10:19,367
If the top degree is at least the bottom
degree, divide first.

139
00:10:20,499 --> 00:10:27,582
Decompose one over x squared minus one: A
over x minus one plus B over x plus one.

140
00:10:28,032 --> 00:10:31,467
A equals one half and B equals negative
one half.

141
00:10:32,599 --> 00:10:39,927
Integrate each piece: one half ln absolute
x minus one, minus one half ln absolute x

142
00:10:39,927 --> 00:10:41,410
plus one, plus C.

143
00:10:41,860 --> 00:10:47,769
Combine: one half ln of the absolute value
of x minus one over x plus one.

144
00:10:48,899 --> 00:10:53,081
The integral from two to three of one over
x squared minus one.

145
00:10:53,531 --> 00:10:59,873
One half times ln of one half minus ln of
one third, which is one half ln three

146
00:10:59,873 --> 00:11:02,683
halves, about point two zero three.

147
00:11:03,833 --> 00:11:09,825
Partial fractions also solves the logistic
differential equation, which we'll meet in

148
00:11:09,825 --> 00:11:10,318
Unit 7.

149
00:11:10,768 --> 00:11:17,680
There, one over P times one minus P splits
into one over P plus one over one minus P.

150
00:11:18,799 --> 00:11:22,319
Your turn. Integrate one over x times x
plus one.

151
00:11:22,769 --> 00:11:24,305
Pause the video and try it.

152
00:11:24,755 --> 00:11:32,767
One over x minus one over x plus one: ln
absolute x minus ln absolute x plus one,

153
00:11:32,767 --> 00:11:33,459
plus C.

154
00:11:34,600 --> 00:11:39,700
In this lesson, a BC topic, we'll
integrate over infinite intervals and

155
00:11:39,700 --> 00:11:41,640
across vertical asymptotes.

156
00:11:42,767 --> 00:11:48,467
The integral from one to infinity of f is
the limit as b goes to infinity of the

157
00:11:48,467 --> 00:11:50,105
integral from one to b.

158
00:11:50,555 --> 00:11:53,649
If that limit is finite, the integral
converges.

159
00:11:54,767 --> 00:12:01,391
The integral from one to infinity of one
over x squared: negative one over x from

160
00:12:01,391 --> 00:12:04,089
one to b is one minus one over b.

161
00:12:04,539 --> 00:12:08,785
As b goes to infinity, that goes to one.
It converges to one.

162
00:12:09,933 --> 00:12:15,949
The integral from one to infinity of one
over x is ln b, which grows forever.

163
00:12:16,399 --> 00:12:19,791
It diverges, even though one over x goes
to zero.

164
00:12:20,933 --> 00:12:25,179
If the integrand blows up inside the
interval, use a limit there too.

165
00:12:25,629 --> 00:12:31,549
The integral from zero to one of one over
root x is the limit of two root x from a

166
00:12:31,549 --> 00:12:34,077
to one, which is two. It converges.

167
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:41,057
The integral from one to infinity of one
over x to the p converges when p is

168
00:12:41,057 --> 00:12:45,141
greater than one, and diverges when p is
at most one.

169
00:12:45,591 --> 00:12:47,981
This p rule will matter again for series.

170
00:12:49,100 --> 00:12:54,689
Your turn. Evaluate the integral from zero
to infinity of e to the negative x.

171
00:12:55,139 --> 00:12:56,675
Pause the video and try it.

172
00:12:57,125 --> 00:13:02,181
The limit of one minus e to the negative b
is one. It converges to one.

